#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125604} :activity/id #uuid "c46f3c61-cb8f-5de3-a323-513dfb71dceb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125863 :tutorial/content "A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0\\in \\mathbb R$ is the set of points that are \"close to\" $x_0.$\n\nMore precisely, let's define a number $\\delta >0$, where $\\delta$ is usually very small. A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0$ is the set of all points whose distance from $x_0$ is smaller than $\\delta.$\n\nIn one-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is an open interval $\\left(x_0-\\delta, x_0+\\delta\\right),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e28aded47a5f57c88b15a05ea032ec05527c0a47a6e5a72f029c5b5d6d9cc61.svg)\n\n![](images/04/04016c37be4c907eabc144db16eb757dff773907262ccd04941cf27ddd2e49d6.png)\n\n![](images/e9/e9455f39338547fa24cf28db5fbf3a925c8dedc6b6d9fe2d1411cc0139f9bdf5.svg)\n\nFor example, if we set $\\delta = 0.1,$ then the corresponding neighborhood of the point $x_0 = 2$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/08/08364d92317893fc21a0af75ccf3c824314e38edbb8a75e23b2206eebf191161.svg)\n\n![](images/03/0329bef56b8b903eb13510b7344fd7ca488d34c8d0c85c7648cebc8ecf7cfa40.png)\n\n![](images/f8/f8bddff4ee56ec85ef47a3f4404de1443a7d2c93491acb1933d97168d5092b7f.svg)\n\nNote that $x=1.9$ and $x=2.1$ are *not* included in this neighborhood since their distance from the point $x_0$ is *not* smaller than $\\delta.$\n\nWe can represent a neighborhood of $x_0$ using set notation as follows:\n\n$$\\{x : | x- x_0| < \\delta \\}$$\n\nWe often refer to sets of this type as *open* neighborhoods since they contain all points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* (but not equal) to $\\delta.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b2ea7b59-5287-5d12-b53f-32a9254b8ba7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6818 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "44c26f5a-57b1-5e3b-b126-e2f92e8f7bac" :step/math-academy-id 41386 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124191 :question/id #uuid "da9684b4-df8d-531d-a200-cf54685067c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10913"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417818}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "13578b7b-3533-5f2d-b6bc-c8214f0f163b" :step/math-academy-id 41387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124192 :question/id #uuid "7e78e3a1-836a-53af-95a3-0a9d192cc7d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10924"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050229}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376110}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "b2201caf-3e1b-53b4-b391-8a27cfbbdce1" :step/math-academy-id 41433 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125864 :tutorial/content "In two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $\\mathbf x_0\\in \\mathbb R^2$ is a disc of radius $\\delta$ centered at $\\mathbf x_0$ that does not include the bounding circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nThe definitions of the interior and boundary points are the same as for the one-dimensional case. The only difference is that now a neighborhood of a point is a disc.\n\nFor example, consider the set $S$ and points $A,$ $B, C,$ and $D,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7e037fd998c235ff7b721e7187d44814700ac5f41a256920f34f248c798c520.png)\n\nLet's classify each of these as interior or boundary points (or neither):\n\n- $A$ is an interior point since *there exists* a neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set.\n- $B$ is neither an interior nor a boundary point because *there exists* a neighborhood of $B$ that does not contain points from the set.\n- $C$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $C$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n- $D$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $D$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n\nNotice that although $C$ and $D$ are both boundary points, $C\\notin S$ and yet $D\\in S.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bfda97a2-2fe7-5c9a-a067-dbf903ba2111" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6825 :tutorial/title "Interior and Boundary Points of Sets in the Plane"} :step/id #uuid "fd03cb93-6f1c-502a-9888-3a2700a9c8a7" :step/math-academy-id 41436 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124199 :question/id #uuid "1f21955b-70dc-5b61-8b19-42860de1be4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-11031"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417817}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "23fe8ae7-b1b8-5490-93e8-49b20cd742f0" :step/math-academy-id 41912 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124200 :question/id #uuid "9b01032c-d4e6-5660-a3c9-477e67c011a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11032"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375645}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "dc8051c9-6e2f-5ded-82a6-e902adddd2c5" :step/math-academy-id 41916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125870 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that $S$ is a set of real numbers. A point $x_0$ is called an **interior point** of $S$ if *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is *fully contained* inside $S.$\n\nFor example, consider the set $S=[0,1)$ and the points $A$ and $B,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/22/22a3429a80227c296fb911ce0caefd282e4066c70da10cf9fe9740a5523a0da7.svg)\n\n![](images/a1/a12c60b6f60ca7c35162fb1ddf0d0d507fe80e73e621c451c21f0b882034ae21.png)\n\n![](images/2e/2e28aded47a5f57c88b15a05ea032ec05527c0a47a6e5a72f029c5b5d6d9cc61.svg)\n\nThe point $A$ is an interior point of $S,$ but $B$ is not:\n\n- The point $A$ is an interior point of $S$ since there exists a neighborhood of $A$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$\n- The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $B$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$ In other words, any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nNote that neither $x=0$ nor $x=1$ are interior points. To see why, let's draw a neighborhood of each point:\n\n![](images/f3/f320e2e063366c71293fb0d5433ccd75ef5794dce9c69f9780c52610823b13a8.svg)\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\nFrom here, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=0$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n- The point $x=1$ is also *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=1$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n\nThe set containing all interior points of a set $S$ is called the **interior** of $S,$ and is denoted $\\textrm{int}\\,S.$\n\nFor the set $[0,1),$ its interior is given by\n\n$$\\textrm{int}\\,S = (0,1).$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "da6d3c21-fe0a-566e-a6e0-117f10988923" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7685 :tutorial/title "Interior Points"} :step/id #uuid "99fecfa4-9502-52e4-aff9-d84b074446bb" :step/math-academy-id 44624 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125871 :tutorial/content "A point $x_0$ is a **boundary point** of $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that *are* in $S$ *and* points that are *not* in $S.$\n\nLet's consider the set $S=[0,1),$ the points $x=0, x=1,$ and neighborhoods of these points.\n\n![](images/81/81d196b07cb70c7440bbf32cd23c2929b0e10f883f5bfdc90b087830dab5fdee.svg)\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\n![](images/22/22a3429a80227c296fb911ce0caefd282e4066c70da10cf9fe9740a5523a0da7.svg)\n\nBoth $x=0$ and $x=1$ are boundary points of $S{:}$\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a boundary point because *every* open neighborhood contains points that belong to $S$ and points that do not belong to $S.$\n- The point $x=1$ is a boundary point because *every* open neighborhood contains points that belong to $S$ and points that do not belong to $S.$\n\nThe set of all boundary points of a set $S$ is called the **boundary** of $S,$ and is denoted as $\\partial S.$\n\nFor the set $[0,1)$ above, its boundary contains two points only, namely $x=0$ and $x=1,$ and we write\n\n$$\\partial S = \\{0,1\\}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "54b0ce40-c312-54e6-87c5-ba2b69af3dae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7686 :tutorial/title "Boundary Points"} :step/id #uuid "2b346783-d47e-5992-9033-dcf4627c7e39" :step/math-academy-id 44625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125606}}] :activity/title "Interior and Boundary Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124723}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417821} :activity/id #uuid "1525b4dc-2341-50b4-9999-2ad38292c9ae" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0972b4fe-a6de-5b28-91e1-b8f15d278acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417785} {:answer/id #uuid "26c9aa3e-4575-55fe-bef6-0e504f1fb60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417789} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd3bade-13d7-5747-b1e8-adac5e633beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417792} {:answer/id #uuid "505ee955-4b02-50a3-9047-a855589dfea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417796} {:answer/id #uuid "b7801bcd-fba9-5c75-b2ca-bfd94012877d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417789} :answer-field/id #uuid "b71b8e04-6ae9-5c68-998d-3aaf22c66038" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4db015a0-682b-5ff4-909b-ad19f05ab168" :question/math-academy-id "q-71826" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b838b945d0b4002d8ae2238af5992c83dcf23b2985c448c716a5caf8de31b369.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/be/be1ece00243777f3f6b6973319e15ea1e5c8556b2452b61a451713f9dcb86595.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'."} :step/id #uuid "05f3aec1-db2e-5c3d-b48a-0200d46f45e8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417822}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e33f924-4eb3-55c2-bd43-bfeaf2283adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417790} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0ca9a9-7bc2-5a65-be19-0f409201b0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417799} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b376f2-838b-5270-8108-30de4fdb1fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417804} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e22708-5653-5d53-b84a-eb8c3f50f2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417807} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4df2d7-22d1-55de-8deb-c49dbe88df60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417804} :answer-field/id #uuid "24ed1e3e-e1e8-525a-96ba-0dbe6634885b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7105850-e717-5535-8a28-db3aaf9f3031" :question/math-academy-id "q-159495" :question/problem "![](images/19/19174ec33e43c900773031043d5de200e892f5e3ee51704d0800b6a944147155.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/43/43362800e91cbb57c22fc6851c10f00927a030a790dea9284e69a5c7c3d4ae25.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."} :step/id #uuid "09199b9d-3f40-5adc-9fbb-571a33f5f584"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4771d2c5-e76c-5c6d-b03d-8fc0e2f1b02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417795} {:answer/id #uuid "8c25eeeb-3961-56fd-8538-a5080285faaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417798} {:answer/id #uuid "b2486cd6-bba5-5ab1-b4ec-64ec7a261726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417802} {:answer/id #uuid "c8abcd16-e7db-5b58-b6bc-d3000736599d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417805} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d9d96b-0d1b-5959-be29-c5d2995ca8f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417806}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417802} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e293cef-2f84-5bc4-9212-2735610d6da3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b10043b-af03-5e4b-8ef7-b7ee2892ed6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36409" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/bb/bbc3e1bfb40c12cfdc655b4207c5f34ea0a01ce254b3f04b96b05ffbbad3f5af.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/10/100a2b2c1aa58cdfa59980cae4253e1de8b4b2c984c0793c66f7e7cc6edb5211.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"} :step/id #uuid "15c853c6-73a1-5b09-aa32-230241fa286d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16604d5a-129d-54c3-83f1-2dbdb9c13b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417786} {:answer/id #uuid "40d9eb9a-aabc-5cf9-8235-dc55fba04a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417793} {:answer/id #uuid "472664cf-4eed-5797-99be-1d353c048466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417794} {:answer/id #uuid "70e7043b-4a4b-5f86-9660-b7c6a4edac74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417797} {:answer/id #uuid "ff04d84b-3945-56d8-abdb-c06778ca1398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417793} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9aa40d2-6ab6-5496-ab09-1db4a0c06d75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dbe53a96-4c4c-5897-ba48-d4861ec6cb49" :question/math-academy-id "q-158547" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y>2x-3},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2x-3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=2x-3.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/5a/5af40a400e5ea4942a1cf89b09184a277df96d252e4a7fee41bd2df7024a631f.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/27/27b978864eac65c93c7886b2d54dfede7130f0f93a16ef96421cd0e5741a6940.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=2x-3.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the coordinates of $Q(0,0)$ satisfy the inequality $y>2x-3.$ Hence, $Q$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"} :step/id #uuid "209dd426-c134-5aa2-9527-59de41410725" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417823}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18dc53b3-03ab-5649-8fb0-de461024a5e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417787} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2bf559-c6e4-56a2-a728-3d0c5adf819f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417788} {:answer/id #uuid "3f689a2a-f4e1-53c0-8a22-5936c1c9857a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417791} {:answer/id #uuid "a71b86c9-6729-5559-b5d7-43ee35ceae47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417800} {:answer/id #uuid "a91a4cd9-65c4-5d08-a8a1-36ab03b7d971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417788} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5bc6201-b1a5-5eb7-b325-cd8c3c42ef38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e4c0564-b5b1-55bc-bc17-3dffa3a951a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158601" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<-1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=-1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,-1)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=-1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/75/75d5d199d12cc2f3170822a40c52762fcc8368caac26a8134661f9ac72ab462b.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/81/818da293be040897d3a536b396d6fe7ff992f3d91f976301e14a6253356b5bde.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=-1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<-1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"} :step/id #uuid "b1e2a497-e042-518c-bac4-15b77f002a69" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417820}}] :activity/title "Interior and Boundary Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417784}]}
